1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,854
Here's a two-minute walkthrough of the
McKinley Resident Assistant,

2
00:00:03,854 --> 00:00:06,443
an AI chat assistant for apartment residents.

3
00:00:07,305 --> 00:00:10,169
It's built for people who live in
a McKinley community,

4
00:00:10,169 --> 00:00:12,148
or are thinking about moving into one.

5
00:00:12,428 --> 00:00:17,347
Today, reporting a broken air conditioner means
calling the office, waiting on hold,

6
00:00:17,347 --> 00:00:20,919
or leaving a voicemail and hoping
someone writes it all down.

7
00:00:21,198 --> 00:00:24,968
The assistant replaces that phone call.
It's open around the clock,

8
00:00:24,968 --> 00:00:28,345
and it turns a quick message into a
complete repair request.

9
00:00:28,623 --> 00:00:31,088
People looking for a new home can use it too,

10
00:00:31,088 --> 00:00:33,444
to ask about communities or request a tour.

11
00:00:33,724 --> 00:00:38,332
We'll follow Marisol, a sample resident with
made-up details, as she signs in.

12
00:00:39,214 --> 00:00:42,926
The input is just a message, written the
way she would say it out loud.

13
00:00:43,636 --> 00:00:47,159
No forms and no special words. One
sentence says what's wrong,

14
00:00:47,159 --> 00:00:49,887
which room, and whether
maintenance may come in.

15
00:00:50,166 --> 00:00:52,364
If she leaves something out, like the room,

16
00:00:52,364 --> 00:00:55,073
the assistant simply asks a
short follow-up question.

17
00:00:55,955 --> 00:00:59,749
The output is a repair request card,
filled in from her message.

18
00:00:59,749 --> 00:01:02,654
The label Ready to send means
nothing is missing.

19
00:01:02,933 --> 00:01:06,415
Each row is one part of the request:
what's wrong, where it is,

20
00:01:06,415 --> 00:01:07,797
and her entry permission.

21
00:01:08,077 --> 00:01:12,574
It also checks her account history. An
earlier AC request is still open,

22
00:01:12,574 --> 00:01:16,696
so the team can see this is a repeat
problem, not a brand-new one.

23
00:01:17,578 --> 00:01:22,313
Nothing is sent on its own. Marisol reviews
the card first. If something's wrong,

24
00:01:22,313 --> 00:01:25,237
she can tap Don't send, or just
type a correction.

25
00:01:25,516 --> 00:01:29,185
And if she'd rather talk to someone, Talk
to a person is always on screen.

26
00:01:29,465 --> 00:01:32,665
When it looks right, she taps Send,
and gets a reference number.

27
00:01:32,944 --> 00:01:36,037
She can tap Check its status at any
time to see where it stands.

28
00:01:36,319 --> 00:01:40,310
Then a McKinley staff member reviews it in
the staff queue, with the room,

29
00:01:40,310 --> 00:01:43,978
the entry permission and the priority,
before anything is scheduled.

30
00:01:44,859 --> 00:01:49,510
One limitation: the assistant isn't connected
to McKinley's maintenance system yet.

31
00:01:49,789 --> 00:01:52,085
Requests are saved in this prototype only,

32
00:01:52,085 --> 00:01:55,912
so staff still enter the work order and
schedule the visit themselves.

33
00:01:56,192 --> 00:01:59,650
So that's the workflow: one message
in, a checked request out,

34
00:01:59,650 --> 00:02:02,997
and a person in the loop at every
step. Thanks for watching.

